Definition: Encapsulation is one of the pillars of OOP. It means wrapping data (fields) and methods (functions) together inside a class and controlling access using access modifiers (private, public, protected).

Purpose: Protects internal state, exposes only what’s necessary, and prevents unintended interference.

Analogy: Think of a capsule medicine 💊 — the outer shell hides the ingredients, but you still get the effect when consumed.

🔑 Example
csharp
public class BankAccount
{
    private double balance; // hidden data

    public void Deposit(double amount)
    {
        balance += amount;
    }

    public double GetBalance()
    {
        return balance; // controlled access
    }
}
📌 ref and out in C#
Encapsulation often pairs with controlled parameter passing.

ref keyword: Passes a variable by reference. The variable must be initialized before being passed.

out keyword: Also passes by reference, but the variable must be assigned inside the method before returning.

Example
csharp
class Demo
{
    public void Add(ref int x, int y)
    {
        x = x + y; // modifies original value
    }

    public void Divide(int a, int b, out int quotient, out int remainder)
    {
        quotient = a / b;
        remainder = a % b; // assigns values before returning
    }
}
🎯 20 Interview Q&A (Encapsulation + ref/out)
Q#	Question	Answer
1	What is encapsulation in C#?	Encapsulation is binding data and methods together inside a class and restricting direct access using access modifiers.
2	Why is encapsulation important?	It improves security, hides implementation details, and provides controlled access.
3	How do access modifiers support encapsulation?	private hides members, public exposes controlled APIs, protected allows subclass access.
4	Give a real-world example of encapsulation.	A car: you use the steering wheel and pedals (public methods), but engine details are hidden (private fields).
5	Difference between encapsulation and abstraction?	Encapsulation = hiding data implementation. Abstraction = hiding complexity by exposing only essential features.
6	Can encapsulation exist without classes?	No, encapsulation is achieved through classes/structs in OOP.
7	How do properties help encapsulation in C#?	Properties (get/set) allow controlled access to private fields.
8	Example of encapsulation using properties?	csharp private int age; public int Age { get { return age; } set { if(value>0) age=value; } }
9	What is the default access modifier in C# class members?	private.
10	How does encapsulation improve maintainability?	Changes inside a class don’t affect external code since only public APIs are exposed.
11	What is ref in C#?	A keyword to pass variables by reference, requiring initialization before use.
12	What is out in C#?	A keyword to pass variables by reference, requiring assignment inside the method before returning.
13	Difference between ref and out?	ref requires initialization before passing; out requires assignment inside the method.
14	Can a method have both ref and out parameters?	Yes, but they serve different purposes.
15	Example of ref usage?	csharp int a=5; Add(ref a,10); // modifies a
16	Example of out usage?	csharp Divide(10,3,out q,out r);
17	Why use ref instead of returning a value?	To modify multiple variables directly without creating extra return types.
18	Why use out instead of returning a value?	To return multiple values from a single method.
19	Can ref and out be used with properties?	No, only with variables.
20	How do ref and out relate to encapsulation?	They allow controlled manipulation of data while still keeping fields private inside classes.

